",\"requirement\":\"Keep seven compressed rotations\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Config specifies `rotate 7` and `compress`; a forced rotation produced app.log.1.gz (compressed) immediately, confirming compression takes effect without a delay cycle. Config persisted post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-45ab240cadc14dbb\",\"cmd-ed6947f18cbf4814\",\"cmd-f2134364d49b4146\"]},{\"id\":\"app-continues-writing\",\"requirement\":\"Allow the application to continue writing to app.log after rotation\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Config uses `copytruncate`, so the log path is never renamed out from under a writer. A forced rotation was directly followed by successful writes to app.log both immediately after rotation and again after the node restart, directly demonstrating continued writability.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ed6947f18cbf4814\",\"cmd-13b0614cc0de4d3f\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id 35c87ec9..., valid config, rotated .gz archive present) was captured, restart was acknowledged via shutdown -r now (SSH session dropped), the node became reachable again with a different boot_id (935253ac...), and post-restart state showed the config intact, the .gz archive intact, a passing dry-run, and a successful new write — a complete pre/during/post transition. Cross-checked by the independent global boot-identity snapshots (after_prepare vs after_executor), which show the same boot_id change.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5d9dc7988e614f1c\",\"cmd-171a3e6be2a04e08\",\"cmd-49e5904132624e1f\",\"cmd-f2134364d49b4146\",\"cmd-13b0614cc0de4d3f\",\"global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Executor mutations were reasonably scoped and mostly self-corrected. cmd-815a57487e8e4f9a was a failed write attempt with no state change (no sudo). cmd-f4e0a837ff6843bf wrote an initial logrotate config (with delaycompress) that was superseded later. cmd-129f8d0f376d4faf performed a test rotation producing an uncompressed app.log.1, which was explicitly removed (`rm -f /var/log/inventory/app.log.1`) in the very next command (cmd-45ab240cadc14dbb) before the final config was validated — a bounded, fully-reverted experiment. cmd-ed6947f18cbf4814 overwrote app.log's content via `tee` (rather than appending) to stage a clean rotation demonstration; this permanently replaced the prepared baseline content (which included an 'initial record count: 2' line) with synthetic test lines. This was a deliberate, bounded, low-risk mutation needed to produce clear final evidence of compression and continued-write behavior, but it discarded original file content that a more careful approach could have preserved. No residue was left in /tmp, no failed services, and no listening-socket or package-health changes attributable to the executor beyond expected reboot artifacts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-815a57487e8e4f9a\",\"cmd-f4e0a837ff6843bf\",\"cmd-129f8d0f376d4faf\",\"cmd-45ab240cadc14dbb\",\"cmd-ed6947f18cbf4814\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the public task — daily rotation, 7 compressed rotations, and continued application writes via copytruncate — are directly demonstrated through a forced rotation showing immediate .gz creation and successful post-rotation writes, with the config confirmed valid via logrotate -d. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also fully and coherently executed: a healthy pre-restart baseline, a restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity, and full post-restart recovery of config, archive, and write capability, corroborated by the independent global boot-identity snapshots. Minor hygiene concern: one test rotation cycle (later cleaned up) and one destructive overwrite of the original app.log content during final-evidence staging, both bounded and non-residual.\",\"limitations\":[\"No evidence of an actual long-lived application process writing to app.log independently of the executor's own test writes — discovery commands found no running service or cron job tied to 'inventory', so continued-writability was demonstrated via manual writes rather than by observing a real application's behavior.\",\"The original prepared app.log content (sha256 3ccbdca...) was overwritten by the executor during test-rotation staging (cmd-ed6947f18cbf4814), so the pre-task log content itself is not preserved in final state, though this does not affect satisfaction of the rotation task.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"daily-rotation","requirement":"Rotate /var/log/inventory/app.log daily","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final /etc/logrotate.d/inventory config contains `daily` directive, confirmed present both before and after node restart via direct file reads and passing logrotate -d dry-run validation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45ab240cadc14dbb","cmd-f2134364d49b4146"]},{"id":"keep-7-compressed","requirement":"Keep seven compressed rotations","status":"satisfied","summary":"Config specifies `rotate 7` and `compress`; a forced rotation produced app.log.1.gz (compressed) immediately, confirming compression takes effect without a delay cycle. Config persisted post-restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45ab240cadc14dbb","cmd-ed6947f18cbf4814","cmd-f2134364d49b4146"]},{"id":"app-continues-writing","requirement":"Allow the application to continue writing to app.log after rotation","status":"satisfied","summary":"Config uses `copytruncate`, so the log path is never renamed out from under a writer. A forced rotation was directly followed by successful writes to app.log both immediately after rotation and again after the node restart, directly demonstrating 